<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Chrodegang of Metz</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. 766</span> · <a href="/centuries/8th-century">8th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Chrodegang was <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/metz">Metz</a> from 742 until his death in 766 and a leading churchman of the early Carolingian kingdom under Pippin the Short. He wrote a rule for clergy living in community that became a model for cathedral canons, and he introduced Roman chant to the Frankish church. He founded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gorze-abbey">Gorze Abbey</a> and briefly headed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lorsch">Lorsch</a> Abbey, and is venerated as a saint.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/chrodegang-of-metz.jpg" alt="Chrodegang of Metz" width="320" height="605" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Graoully/Archimatth · CC BY 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Chrodegang.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Chrodegang was born early in the 8th century in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hesbaye">Hesbaye</a>, around the old Roman civitas of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tongeren">Tongeren</a>, into a noble Frankish family. He was possibly the son of Sigramnus, Count of Hesbaye, and of Landrada, a sister of Rotrude of Hesbaye, the first wife of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-martel">Charles Martel</a>. He was educated at the abbey of Saint-Trond and at the cathedral school of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/metz">Metz</a>, and he is claimed as a forebear of the Frankish dynasty of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/robertians">Robertians</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>At the court of his kinsman <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-martel">Charles Martel</a> he became referendary, then chancellor, and in 737 prime minister, and he kept his civil office after he succeeded Sigibald as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/metz">Metz</a>. In 753 he met and escorted <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-stephen-ii">Pope Stephen II</a>, who had come to seek help against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-lombards">Lombard</a> incursions; as apostolic delegate he took part in the coronation of Pepin in 754 and the defeat of the Lombard king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aistulf">Aistulf</a>. After the death of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saint-boniface">Saint Boniface</a> the pope gave him the pallium, making him an archbishop without raising the see of Metz.</p><p>He founded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gorze-abbey">Gorze Abbey</a> near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/metz">Metz</a> in 748 and St. Peter's Abbey on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moselle">Moselle</a>, helped the abbeys of Gengenbach and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lorsch">Lorsch</a>, and is said to have obtained relics of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saint-nazarius">Saint Nazarius</a> for Lorsch and of Saint Gorgonius for Gorze. Around 755 he wrote the Regula Canonicorum, a rule of thirty-four chapters based on the Rule of Saint Benedict but adapted to canons who served the faithful. In 762, during a dangerous illness, he set up among his priests a confraternity of prayer, the League of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/attigny">Attigny</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Chrodegang died at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/metz">Metz</a> on 6 March 766 and was buried at Gorze, the site of his principal shrine. The historian M. A. Claussen holds that his work lay at the foundation of the Carolingian spiritual revival. His rule spread community life among the secular clergy, was partly taken into the Council of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aachen">Aachen</a>'s rule for canons in 816, and gave way only in the eleventh and twelfth centuries to rules based on Augustine. It was probably carried to Ireland, where the Rule of the Céli Dé is analogous to it. He is venerated as a saint in the Catholic and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-orthodox-church">Orthodox</a> churches.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Chrodegang” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Lorsch was founded in 764 by the Frankish count Cancor and his mother Williswinda, and handed to Archbishop <b>Chrodegang of Metz</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/imperial-abbey-of-lorsch">Imperial Abbey of Lorsch</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tongeren">Tongeren</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/belgium">Belgium</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/metz">Metz</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/france">France</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=50.78,5.46&amp;year=756" aria-label="Tongeren on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4674 402 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Chrodegang of Metz"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5124 672h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5124 672h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=50.78,5.46&amp;year=756">What was here in 756 CE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
